#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1cb0c5a-9862-57eb-b57f-acb23890d677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375869} {:answer/id #uuid "62e36acb-5f50-5c64-b175-8079a61023a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375870} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dbbdb6-befc-5ff1-81d4-cba020f3cb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375871} {:answer/id #uuid "13addc19-500d-5e0f-acbb-8b63b95f8faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375872} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fae7f-e95c-5979-8992-c80d112c68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fae7f-e95c-5979-8992-c80d112c68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873} :answer-field/id #uuid "6215ee61-981f-5e63-acd6-8f36d8ca0439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1f2ddde-40be-5a46-987b-92cfeb3c2c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-127321" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-1⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
